==========================================================================
(2) A_5 is PERFECT, via overlapping 3-cycles (the 5-point obstruction)
==========================================================================
 <(123),(345)> has order 60 (=60 => A_5)
 [A_5,A_5] order 60  =>  A_5 perfect: True
 [(123),(345)] = [(0, 3, 2)]  (a nontrivial even perm = commutator; 3-cycles ARE commutators in A_5)

==========================================================================
(3) Pairs of 3-subsets of [n] sharing EXACTLY one element — first nonzero at n=5
==========================================================================
 n=3:    0 pairs of triangles meeting in one vertex  (need 3+3-1=5 points)
 n=4:    0 pairs of triangles meeting in one vertex  (need 3+3-1=5 points)
 n=5:   15 pairs of triangles meeting in one vertex  (need 3+3-1=5 points)
 n=6:   90 pairs of triangles meeting in one vertex  (need 3+3-1=5 points)
 n=7:  315 pairs of triangles meeting in one vertex  (need 3+3-1=5 points)
 n=8:  840 pairs of triangles meeting in one vertex  (need 3+3-1=5 points)

==========================================================================
(4) TOURNAMENT MAP: cyclic triangles (3-cycles) on 5 vertices sharing one vertex
==========================================================================
 round C_3: 1/1 triples are CYCLIC triangles (3-cycles); C_5 is the smallest round tournament where two 3-cycles can share exactly one vertex
 round C_5: 5/10 triples are CYCLIC triangles (3-cycles); C_5 is the smallest round tournament where two 3-cycles can share exactly one vertex
 round C_7: 14/35 triples are CYCLIC triangles (3-cycles); C_5 is the smallest round tournament where two 3-cycles can share exactly one vertex

=> the 'no quintic' obstruction (A_5 perfect, two overlapping triangles) FIRST appears at 5
   points = 5 runners = the round tournament C_5 = the LRC n=5 cyclotomic worry-set witness.
